 FULL_FILE_EXAMPLE = """
-Before editing, decompose the issue into atomic, externally observable requirements, including stated behavior, implied compatibility constraints, and every named or implied edge case. Create a requirement-and-contract matrix with one row per requirement. Record: expected behavior, current behavior, relevant implementation symbols, all callers and consumers, files that may need modification, a concrete pre-fix/post-fix test, required invariants, backward-compatibility constraints, error and boundary semantics, and behaviors that must remain unchanged. Do not treat a plausible implementation as sufficient: every row must identify the observable contract and the code path that enforces it. Before editing, explicitly list likely hidden-test variations for each row and ensure the planned patch addresses them without broadening behavior beyond the issue. Treat unverified requirements as unresolved rather than assuming the main path is sufficient. For each requirement, trace the relevant call path from the public entry point through the implementation and record which code path must change and which existing behavior must remain unchanged. Do not implement until every requirement has a concrete verification plan. After each edit, execute the highest-risk tests first, inspect the complete failure output and traceback, and classify every failure as an implementation defect, test defect, environment issue, or unrelated regression. If a test is weak, add a sharper assertion before proceeding. Do not declare success from a partial pass: rerun the full requirement matrix and at least one regression suite after the final change. For every independently stated behavior, identify the implementation location, all relevant callers and consumers, and the compatibility contract they rely on. Before editing, inspect each caller's assumptions about types, exceptions, defaults, ordering, mutation, and serialization. Explicitly state which existing behaviors must remain unchanged and explain why the proposed edit preserves them or why a deliberate change is required. Run a test matrix for every requirement: a representative ordinary case, the smallest and largest meaningful boundary cases, malformed or ambiguous inputs, a regression test for the reported failure, and at least one downstream consumer or serialization/API boundary. Include both positive and negative assertions, and make each test distinguish the intended fix from a superficially plausible implementation. when the changed value crosses a module boundary. Explain why the patch preserves unaffected callers before approval. Each test must assert the exact expected value, exception, state transition, or externally visible side effect—not merely that execution completes—and must exercise the public entry point used by the issue. For every failing test, classify the failure as an implementation defect, an incorrect expectation, an invalid test setup, or an environment problem; then make the smallest justified change and rerun the failed test plus the full affected requirement set. Never replace a failing assertion with a weaker existence or smoke check. Maintain a requirement-to-test matrix with one row per atomic requirement and columns for public entry point, input partition, expected result, observed result, and evidence. Mark each row PASS, FAIL, UNTESTED, or BLOCKED; never treat UNTESTED, BLOCKED, an infrastructure error, an empty test selection, or an ambiguous result as approval. Before finalizing, verify that every row has executed evidence and that boundary, malformed-input, regression, and unaffected-behavior partitions are represented where applicable. Do not approve a patch while any requirement lacks a directly relevant test or while a test result is ambiguous. For each surviving candidate, actively search for a counterexample: test the reported failure, the nearest boundary on both sides, invalid and empty inputs, repeated or reordered calls, and at least one previously valid use case. Prefer the candidate that survives the same adversarial checks with the fewest unrelated changes; if candidates tie or all have unresolved counterexamples, generate or revise a candidate rather than guessing. When multiple candidate patches are available, score each candidate independently against every requirement and consumer audit using only observed evidence: required behavior passes, regression behavior passes, boundary and malformed-input behavior passes, unaffected behavior remains intact, and the diff is minimal and localized. Reject any candidate with a failing or untested required row; do not select a candidate because it is more eloquent, more complex, or preferred by a majority when its evidence is weaker. Prefer a candidate only when it has direct passing evidence for every requirement, no unexplained regressions, and a clear causal explanation for the reported failure; reject candidates that pass only generic tests, rely on unverified assumptions, or leave any requirement unresolved. After each test run, map every failure, unexpected pass, skipped test, warning, and traceback back to a specific matrix requirement. For each result, record the observed behavior, expected behavior, likely root cause, and the next code or test change. Re-run the smallest reproducer after every revision, then re-run the full relevant suite. An unexplained failure, skipped requirement, or test whose assertion does not distinguish the buggy behavior from the fixed behavior blocks approval.
